    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#louis-the-german"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-the-german.jpg" alt="Louis the German" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#louis-the-german">Louis the German</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwig_der_Deutsche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zürich stands where the Limmat leaves Lake Zürich. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Romans</a> kept a customs post there, Turicum, on the route between Gaul and Raetia, and a late Roman fort stood on the Lindenhof hill. In the Carolingian period the town grew around two religious foundations: the Grossmünster, associated with the cult of the martyrs Felix and Regula, and the Fraumünster, a convent founded in 853 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, whose abbess became the nominal lady of the town. From the late eleventh century the dukes of Zähringen held the imperial advocacy over Zürich, and under their protection the town prospered as a market on the routes towards the Alpine passes. When the Zähringen line died out in 1218, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> did not grant the advocacy to another lord, and Zürich came directly under the empire as an imperial city. A council of knights and merchants governed it, gradually displacing the abbess. In 1336 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rudolf-brun">Rudolf Brun</a>, a member of the council, led craftsmen and excluded families in overthrowing the old council and introduced a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guild</a> constitution under which he ruled as burgomaster for life. Threatened by exiled councillors, who attempted a coup in 1350 with the support of the Count of Habsburg-Laufenburg at Rapperswil, Brun concluded a perpetual alliance with Lucerne, Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden on 1 May 1351, bringing Zürich into the Old Swiss Confederacy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval Zürich was a city of merchants and craftsmen, known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> weaving in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries and for trade on the lake and the Limmat, while its rural territory produced grain, wine and dairy goods. After 1336 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guilds</a> organised economic life, regulating crafts and prices, while the Constaffel brought together the old patrician families. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> reshaped society: the monasteries were dissolved and their wealth used for poor relief and schooling, a marriage court supervised morals, and the old chapter school of the Grossmünster was refounded in 1525 as a theological college, the Prophezei. In the sixteenth century Protestant refugees from Locarno, among them the Muralt and Orelli families, revived silk manufacture, and Huguenot refugees later reinforced it. From the seventeenth century city merchants organised a putting-out system in which thousands of rural households, especially in the hilly Zürich Oberland, spun and wove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cotton">cotton</a> and silk, making the countryside one of the most industrialised regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> before mechanisation. Wealth and political rights nonetheless remained concentrated among the citizens of the city, who monopolised trade and office, while the rural population, the great majority, were subjects without a voice in government. Literacy was relatively high, and the Reformed church supervised schooling and conduct through its pastors.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#old-zurich-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-zurich-war.jpg" alt="Old Zürich War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#old-zurich-war">Old Zürich War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Alter_Zuerichkrieg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marco Zanoli (sidonius 20:10, 23 August 2006 (UTC)), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zürich’s forces were based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guild</a> militias of the city and the levies of its rural districts, raised by the councils and led by members of the leading families. The city withstood a siege by Duke Albert II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a> in 1351–1354. Its most serious defeat came in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#old-zurich-war">Old Zürich War</a> of 1440–1446, when Zürich, in conflict with Schwyz over the inheritance of the last Count of Toggenburg, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> King Frederick III against the other confederates. The confederates defeated the Zürich army at the Battle of St. Jakob an der Sihl in 1443, where the burgomaster Rudolf Stüssi was killed, and besieged the city; in the peace settlement of 1450 Zürich had to abandon the Habsburg alliance. Zürich contingents fought alongside the other cantons in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundian-wars">Burgundian Wars</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hans-waldmann">Hans Waldmann</a> commanding Zürich’s troops at the Battle of Morat in 1476, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> until the defeat at Marignano in 1515. Zwingli’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenary</a> service, and religious division led to civil war: in 1531 the Catholic cantons defeated Zürich at the Battle of Kappel, where Zwingli himself fell. The Catholic cantons won again in the First War of Villmergen in 1656, but Zürich and Bern were victorious in the Second War of Villmergen in 1712. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a> Zürich stayed neutral and built new bastioned fortifications from 1642.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty.jpg" alt="Habsburg dynasty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Counts_of_Habsbourg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Brun’s constitution and its later revisions, Zürich was ruled by its citizens through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guilds</a>. The craft guilds, eventually twelve, and the patrician Constaffel sent representatives to the Small Council, which handled daily government and justice, and to the Large Council of two hundred, which decided on war, alliances and taxation; two burgomasters alternated in office every half year. The rural territory, built up in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries by purchase, pledge and conquest from indebted nobles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, included the county of Kyburg, pledged to Zürich in 1424, and the town of Winterthur, acquired in 1467. It was governed by bailiffs drawn from the city’s council families, who resided in castles such as Kyburg and Grüningen, while villages kept their own communal assemblies and customary rights. Discontent sometimes erupted, as in 1489, when peasant protests and guild opposition led to the overthrow and execution of the burgomaster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hans-waldmann">Hans Waldmann</a>, and in the tax revolts of 1645–1646 around Wädenswil. Within the Confederacy Zürich held the leading position as the <em>Vorort</em>, convening the federal Diet and conducting common business, and it shared with other cantons the government of common lordships such as Baden and the Thurgau. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the council also governed the church, appointing pastors and enforcing moral discipline.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#huldrych-zwingli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli.jpg" alt="Huldrych Zwingli" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulrich-Zwingli-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hans Asper, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zürich’s greatest influence was religious. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>, people’s priest at the Grossmünster from 1519, led the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the Swiss Confederacy: after the public disputation of January 1523 the council adopted his programme, images were removed from the churches and in 1525 the Mass was replaced by a simple communion service. Zwingli’s symbolic understanding of the Eucharist, which he defended against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> at the Marburg Colloquy of 1529, became a defining feature of Reformed Protestantism. His successor Heinrich Bullinger, head of the Zürich church from 1531 to 1575, corresponded across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, agreed the Consensus Tigurinus with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-calvin">John Calvin</a> in 1549 and wrote the Second Helvetic Confession of 1566, adopted by Reformed churches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hungary">Hungary</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scotland">Scotland</a>. Zürich was also the birthplace of the Anabaptist movement, whose first adult baptisms took place there in 1525 and whose leaders were persecuted; Felix Manz was drowned in the Limmat in 1527. In the eighteenth century the city was a centre of the German-language <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, home of the critics Johann Jakob Bodmer and Johann Jakob Breitinger, the poet Salomon Gessner and the writer Johann Caspar Lavater, and birthplace of the educator Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cotton">cotton</a> trades, and the merchant capital they generated, laid the foundations of later Swiss industry and banking.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The old regime rested on the privileges of the city’s citizens over a large and increasingly prosperous rural population, and in the late eighteenth century that arrangement came under strain. In 1794–1795 the Stäfa Memorial, a petition from villagers on the lake demanding the restoration of old rural liberties and equal economic rights, was answered with the military occupation of Stäfa, arrests and harsh sentences. The French invasion of the Confederacy in early 1798 brought the system down. As French armies defeated Bern, the Zürich government granted equality to its rural subjects in February 1798 and soon gave way to a provisional government, and the canton became part of the centralised Helvetic Republic proclaimed in April. In 1799 Zürich was a battlefield in the War of the Second Coalition, when French forces under André Masséna fought Austrian and then Russian armies in the two Battles of Zürich. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a> of 1803 restored Zürich as a canton of the Swiss Confederation, now with rural representation, and the liberal movement launched at the Ustertag of 1830 brought equal rights for town and country. Zürich founded a university in 1833, became the seat of the Federal Polytechnic in 1855 and grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#switzerland">Switzerland</a>’s largest city and financial centre, and the canton remains the most populous in Switzerland.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The city-republic of Zürich, an imperial city that joined the Swiss Confederacy in 1351 and came to lead it, ruled a large rural territory through its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guilds</a> and councils and, under Zwingli and Bullinger, became a fountainhead of Reformed Protestantism whose influence reached across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and beyond.</em></p>

